KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17 --object-bits 12
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ conversion op to vector: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
Currently: "found no match for symbol 'C'" resolving the functional
cast to the deduced parameter inside the instantiated operator.
ROUND-96 LOCALIZATION (sharp): the failure belongs to the CONVERSION
OPERATOR path, not to functional casts or deduced parameters:
  * `C tmp; tmp.push_back(*begin()); return tmp;` inside the SAME
    operator works, so C really is std::vector<int> there;
  * `return C();` inside the operator works;
  * the identical cast `return C(begin(), end());` in a plain MEMBER
    TEMPLATE `template <class C> C to() const` on the same class
    VERIFIES (kernel fc6);
  * `C(b, e)` in a free function template `template <class C> C make(...)`
    verifies (fc4), and `std::vector<int> v(arr, arr+3)` verifies (fc3).
Only in the conversion operator does constructor lookup go wrong, and
the candidates reported are `std::allocator`'s constructors -- i.e. the
lookup scopes into the LAST template argument of vector<int,
allocator<int>> instead of vector itself ([class.conv.fct] +
[over.match.ctor]).  A one-line workaround exists in user code (use a
named member template), which is why this layer only surfaces through
libc++/CBMC-style ranges adaptors.
ROUND-96 reduction (cvise with a real g++ -Werror gate): `sum` needs no
BODY -- a declaration suffices, so the failure is purely in converting
`ranget` to `const std::vector<int>&` at the call.  (With no body the
expected verdict is a nondet result, so this test asserts the FRONT END
resolves the conversion: currently it reports "found no match for
symbol 'C'".)  A hand-written vector-like target with default, copy and
SFINAE-free iterator-pair constructors does NOT reproduce (kernel fc11),
so libstdc++'s real vector -- most likely its constrained iterator-pair
constructor -- is required.
